-- Gezira's C build and its native image, twinned (check/laws/gezira-twin.l): a chain of kernels run by both on the same
-- input agrees when the outputs have the same count and every float is within the band (1e-5 of the larger magnitude,
-- at least 1e-6).  The engine draws each case, parameters and input values as integers (each k the float k/16), from a
-- seed native_nile makes fresh each run and prints (MARU_LAW_SEED replays a refutation); the edge cases (edge values,
-- buffer-crossing lengths) are run whole every time.  twin_detects is the twin's own check: with the native side's first
-- parameter moved by 1e-3 it must disagree, so a twin that compared a build with itself, or never looked at the floats,
-- fails here.
under test: twin, twin_perturbed, twin_edges
let numerals = [-256 .. 256]
defaults trials = 100, size = 6

rule params6: number(a), number(b), number(c), number(d), number(e), number(f) => params6([a, b, c, d, e, f])
rule points_one: number(x), number(y) => points([x, y])
rule points_more: number(x), number(y), points(ps) => points([x, y | ps])

law twin_transform_points: all ps: params6(ps), xs: points(xs). twin(["TransformPoints"], ps, xs, 1) = 0
law twin_transform_points_edges: all w in [1, 4]. twin_edges(["TransformPoints"], w) = 0 by exhaustion
law twin_detects: all ps: params6(ps), xs: points(xs). twin_perturbed(["TransformPoints"], ps, xs, 1) = 1
